The '''2010 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2009–10 UEFA Europa League]], the first season of the revamped European [[association football|football]] competition formerly known as the UEFA Cup. Played at the [[Volksparkstadion]] in [[Hamburg]], Germany, on 12 May 2010,<ref name="exec_meet">{{Cite news|title=Madrid and Hamburg awarded 2010 finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/01cb-0f84d3be8863-01dcdabe82b3-1000--madrid-and-hamburg-awarded-2010-finals/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 March 2008 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100323040748/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/aboutuefa/organisation/executivecommittee/news/newsid%3D676743.html |archive-date=23 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> the match was won by [[Spain]]'s [[Atlético Madrid]], who, after extra time, beat [[England]]'s [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]] 2–1.<ref name="bbc_report">{{Cite news|first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Atletico Madrid 2–1 Fulham |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8675486.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=12 May 2010 |access-date=13 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100513033124/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8675486.stm |archive-date=13 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The win gave Atlético their second major European title, following the [[1961–62 European Cup Winners' Cup]]. Having beaten defending champions [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] on the way, Fulham were playing in their first final in only their second season of European football, and their second major final overall in the club's history.

As the winners, Atlético qualified automatically for the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League]]. They also played in the [[2010 UEFA Super Cup]] in [[Monaco]] on 27 August 2010, where they took on Italy's [[Inter Milan]], the winners of the [[2009–10 UEFA Champions League]]; Atlético won 2–0.

==Background==
Atlético Madrid and Fulham had never played against each other before this match, and Atlético were Fulham's first ever Spanish opponents. Atlético, on the other hand, had played 19 previous matches against English opposition, winning six and losing five; the most recent of these was against [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the semi-finals.<ref name="background">{{Cite news|title=Hamburg showdown awaits Atlético and Fulham |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e5-0e7474a8d8ff-a850d87e6549-1000--hamburg-showdown-awaits-atletico-and-fulham/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=3 May 2010 |access-date=13 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100511194133/http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/matches/season%3D2010/round%3D2000042/match%3D2000967/prematch/background/index.html |archive-date=11 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Fulham were playing in only their second season of European football; in 2002–03, they won the [[2002 UEFA Intertoto Cup|Intertoto Cup]] and reached the third round of the [[2002–03 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]] before being knocked out by [[Hertha BSC]]. Atlético, however, had played in four previous European finals, including the [[1974 European Cup Final]], which they lost to [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], and three [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals. Their only European title came in [[1962 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1962]], when they beat [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] 3–0 in a replay at [[Neckarstadion]], [[Stuttgart]], after the original match at [[Hampden Park]], [[Glasgow]], finished as a 1–1 draw.<ref name="background"/>

The final was Fulham's last chance at qualification for European football in the following season. The [[2009–10 Premier League|Premier League]] season had finished the previous weekend with Fulham finishing in 12th place, 17 points away from the Europa League places. Earlier in the season, they had been knocked out of the [[2009–10 Football League Cup|League Cup]] in the third round and in the sixth round of the [[2009–10 FA Cup|FA Cup]]. Atlético were also unable to qualify for Europe via the league; they went into the match in ninth place in the [[2009–10 La Liga|Priméra División]], eight points away from the league's Europa League places with one game left to play. However, they had a chance at qualification through the [[2009–10 Copa del Rey|Copa del Rey]]; they played against [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] in the [[2010 Copa del Rey Final|final]] on 19 May, but lost 2–0.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Sevilla rain on Atlético's cup parade |url=http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=esp/news/newsid=1490220.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=19 May 2010 |access-date=21 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100524163726/http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association%3Desp/news/newsid%3D1490220.html |archive-date=24 May 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:AOL-Arena.jpg|thumb|The [[Volksparkstadion]], renamed to "Hamburg Arena" for the match, was selected as the venue for the 2010 final in March 2008.]]
The [[Volksparkstadion]] was chosen as the venue for the 2010 UEFA Europa League Final at a meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee in [[Vaduz]], [[Liechtenstein]], on 28 March 2008. The committee – who selected the venue for the [[2010 UEFA Champions League Final]] at the same meeting – based their decision on a number of key criteria, including stadium capacity, facilities and security.<ref name="exec_meet"/>

Before 2010, only two European cup finals had ever been played in the city of [[Hamburg]], both of them at the ''Volksparkstadion'' ({{langx|en|the People's Park Stadium}}), when the finals of certain competitions were played over two legs on a home-and-away basis. The first was the [[1982 UEFA Cup Final]] second leg where hosts [[Hamburger SV]] lost 3–0 to hand the UEFA Cup to [[IFK Göteborg]] of Sweden following a 1–0 defeat in the first leg at [[Ullevi]] in [[Gothenburg]]. The second one was the [[1983 European Super Cup]] first leg where the [[1983 European Cup Final|1983 European Cup]] winners Hamburg drew 0–0 with the [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1983 European Cup Winners' Cup]] winners [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]].

A total of six [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winners|European Cup/UEFA Champions League finals]] and three [[List of UEFA Cup Winners' Cup winners|UEFA Cup Winners' Cup finals]] have been played in German cities, as well as one [[List of UEFA Cup and Europa League winners|UEFA Cup final]] since it became a single-legged match.<ref name="background" />

The Volksparkstadion was opened in 2000 on the site of Hamburg's previous stadia: the Bahrenfelder Stadium and the original Volksparkstadion. The Bahrenfelder Stadium was built in 1925, but after a two-year renovation, it was replaced by the 75,000-capacity Volksparkstadion in 1953. [[Hamburger SV]] moved into the Volksparkstadion from their previous home at [[Sportplatz at Rothenbaum|Rothenbaum]] in 1963. The stadium then played host to three Group 1 matches at the [[1974 FIFA World Cup]] in [[West Germany]]. 14 years later, [[UEFA Euro 1988]] was held in West Germany and the Volksparkstadion was chosen to host the semi-final between [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] and the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]]. By the late 1990s, plans were laid down for a complete renovation of the stadium; the playing surface was rotated by 90 degrees to take advantage of the natural angle of sunlight and the stadium was rebuilt to hold more than 57,000 spectators. Building work began in 1998 and Hamburger SV returned to the ground in 2000. The stadium was used as a venue for the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]], playing host to four group stage matches and the quarter-final between [[Italy national football team|Italy]] and [[Ukraine national football team|Ukraine]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hamburg Arena |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/stadium/index.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 January 2010 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100328075718/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/stadium/index.html |archive-date= 28 March 2010 |url-status= dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Previous finals in the city |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=12 December 2009 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100326060124/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |archive-date= 26 March 2010 |url-status= dead}}</ref>

The 'hosts' for the final, Hamburger SV were only eliminated from the tournament by Fulham at the semi-final stage. The disappointment of coming so close to playing a final at home was doubled for the Hamburg fans as this was the second consecutive year in which they had been knocked out at the same stage in the UEFA Cup/Europa League – in 2008–09, they lost out to local rivals [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]]. Two recent finals had involved a team playing at their usual stadium: [[Feyenoord]] defeated [[Borussia Dortmund]] in Rotterdam in 2002, while [[Sporting CP]] lost to [[PFC CSKA Moscow|CSKA Moscow]] in [[Lisbon]] in 2005.

