==Pre-match==
[[File:Avropa Liqası Muzeyi Bakıda 34.jpg|thumb|left|Match ball]]

===Identity===
Like the recent finals of the UEFA Champions League and UEFA Cup, the 2010 Europa League final was given its own unique visual identity. The design concept is intended to give the public a distinctive impression of the host city and will be used on all media associated with the event. The logo for the 2010 final was revealed at the EAST Hotel Cinema in Hamburg on 30 November 2009 – to coincide with the opening of ticket sales for German residents on 1 December – and the ceremony was attended by Karin Von Welck, the regional officer for sport and culture, [[German Football Association]] general secretary [[Wolfgang Niersbach]], Hamburger SV chairman [[Bernd Hoffmann]] and [[Germany national under-19 football team|Germany Under-19]] coach [[Horst Hrubesch]]. The logo features an artistic impression of two footballers competing for a ball, set against a modern drawing of various elements of the Hamburg skyline.<ref name="logo_launch">{{Cite news|title=Logo launch heralds Hamburg final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01df-0e73689134dc-76740ac0e86a-1000--logo-launch-heralds-hamburg-final/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 November 2009 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100331000406/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D925815.html |archive-date=31 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Related events===
A trophy handover ceremony was held at the ''Fischauktionshalle'' in Hamburg on 13 April 2010, at which a delegation from holders [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] – including chief executive [[Serhiy Palkin]], coach [[Mircea Lucescu]], defender [[Mariusz Lewandowski]] and midfielder [[Jádson]] – returned the trophy to UEFA President [[Michel Platini]]. Platini then presented the trophy to the mayor of Hamburg, [[Ole von Beust]], for it to be put on display in the city until the day of the final. Also present at the ceremony were the presidents of Hamburger SV and the German Football Association: Bernd Hoffmann and [[Theo Zwanziger]].<ref>{{Cite news|title=Hamburg ready for trophy handover ceremony |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/newsid=1474540.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=8 April 2010 |access-date=9 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100410033050/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/newsid%3D1474540.html |archive-date=10 April 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|first=Tim |last=Vollmer |title=Trophy handover in Hamburg |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e4-0e7456ae7971-bc4f67a9cc9f-1000--handover-starts-hamburg-final-countdown/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 April 2010 |access-date=15 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100416183226/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D1476306.html |archive-date=16 April 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Ambassador===
Former Hamburg player [[Uwe Seeler]] was appointed as ambassador of the final.

===Ticketing===
The regular capacity of the Volksparkstadion was just over 57,000; however, for the 2010 Europa League final and other international matches, the standing area in the north stand was converted to seating, reducing the capacity to around 51,500. Approximately 25% of the available tickets were allocated to each club for distribution to their own fans,<ref>{{Cite web|title=UEFA Europa League Final 2010 – FAQ |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/Publications/competitions/Ticketing/01/44/80/00/1448000_DOWNLOAD.pdf|work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100331005506/http://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/Publications/competitions/Ticketing/01/44/80/00/1448000_DOWNLOAD.pdf |archive-date=31 March 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref> while approximately 5,100 tickets were retained for the international general public. Following the ticket application process for German residents – which ran from 1 December 2009 to 15 January 2010<ref name="logo_launch"/><ref>{{Cite news|first=Lars |last=Wellbrock |title=Hamburg hosts hold on to final promise |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e0-0e736dcb4e98-4f1f359c3aec-1000--hamburg-hosts-hold-on-to-final-promise/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Association |date=1 December 2009 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100301040234/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D926961.html |archive-date=1 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> – applications were opened to the international general public on 24 February 2010 and closed on 19 March. As demand exceeded the number of tickets available, successful applications were determined by a ballot at the end of March.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Europa League final tickets on sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e2-0e73e2caa23b-09c71b7f07bb-1000--europa-league-final-tickets-on-sale/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 February 2010 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100408215518/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D1453147.html |archive-date=8 April 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=Europa League final ticket sales close |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e3-0e741f91eabe-f4b4cfdb9e1a-1000--europa-league-final-ticket-sales-close/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=19 March 2010 |access-date=23 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100322202010/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D1465025.html |archive-date=22 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Officials===
The referee for the 2010 UEFA Europa League Final was [[Nicola Rizzoli]], representing the [[Italian Football Federation]].<ref name="referee"/> Rizzoli had been an international referee since 2007, and took charge of his first [[UEFA Champions League]] match in the August of that year, even before his first UEFA Cup match.<ref name="referee"/> Since then, he refereed a further 10 Champions League matches and nine UEFA Cup/Europa League matches. His only Europa League assignment in 2009–10 – other than the final – was the second leg of [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]'s round of 16 tie against [[Lille OSC|Lille]] at [[Anfield]] on 18 March 2010. Rizzoli had previously refereed quarter-finals in both the Champions League and the UEFA Cup.<ref>{{Cite news|first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man Utd 3–2 Bayern Munich (agg 4–4) |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8604742.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=7 April 2010 |access-date=10 May 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100424071429/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8604742.stm |archive-date=24 April 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|first=Nabil |last=Hassan |title=Man City 2–1 Hamburg (3–4) |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/7977083.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=16 April 2009 |access-date=10 May 2010 }}</ref>

The refereeing team was entirely made up of officials from the same country; Rizzoli was joined by assistant referees Cristiano Copelli and Luca Maggiani, and the fourth official was Gianluca Rocchi.<ref name="referee"/> As part of an ongoing experiment throughout the entire 2009–10 UEFA Europa League, there were also two additional assistant referees with the task of monitoring each penalty area; the extra officials for the 2010 final were Paolo Tagliavento and Andrea De Marco.<ref name="referee"/>

===Team selection===
[[File:Fulham vs Athletico Madrid (pre-match ceremony 1).jpg|thumb|The opening ceremony took place immediately before the teams emerged.]]

For Atlético, only [[Sergio Asenjo]] was unavailable through injury; the Spanish back-up goalkeeper started Atlético's match away to [[Sporting de Gijón]] on 8 May after manager [[Quique Sánchez Flores]] rested his regular number 1, [[David de Gea]], ahead of the Europa League final, but he tore ligaments in his right knee after 13 minutes and was ruled out for six months. As well as De Gea, Flores rested a further six regular first-team players for their penultimate league match: [[Luis Amaranto Perea|Luis Perea]], [[Paulo Assunção]], [[Simão Sabrosa|Simão]], [[Raúl García (footballer, born 1986)|Raúl García]], [[Diego Forlán]], and [[Sergio Agüero]].<ref name="match_facts">{{Cite news |title=Match facts: Atlético v Fulham |url=http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid=1486021.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130205040630/http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid=1486021.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 February 2013 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 May 2010 |access-date=14 May 2010 }}</ref>

In the Fulham camp, the most severe injury concerns related to top-scorer [[Bobby Zamora]] and winger [[Damien Duff]]; Zamora strained his Achilles tendon in the second leg of Fulham's semi-final against [[Hamburger SV]] on 29 April and missed the last three league games of the season, while Duff picked up a calf injury in the penultimate league game against [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]] on 5 May. Other players with minor injuries included [[Paul Konchesky]] (ankle), [[Brede Hangeland]] (knee), [[Aaron Hughes]] (groin), [[John Paintsil]] (muscle strain), and [[Bjørn Helge Riise]] (stomach cramps).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/2010/0510/264893-hodgsonr/ |title=Hodgson hoping Fulham issues will ease |work=[[RTÉ]] |date=10 May 2010 |access-date=10 February 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/2010/0511/264954-fulham/ |title=Cottagers sweating over Zamora and Duff |work=[[RTÉ]] |date=11 May 2010 |access-date=10 February 2025}}</ref>

Neither team had any players missing through suspension, so the final team selections were largely as expected; the biggest surprise was from Fulham manager [[Roy Hodgson]], who named [[Chris Baird]] at right-back ahead of Ghanaian international [[John Paintsil]].<ref>{{Cite news|first=Paul |last=Hayward |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/may/12/roy-hodgson-fulham-europa-league |title=Fulham's Roy Hodgson flies the flag for the radical English coach |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=12 May 2010 }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Atlético Madrid Europa League 2010 Pennant.jpg|thumb|Atlético's pre-match pennant]]
Atlético started the stronger side and had a chance to take the lead in the 12th minute when [[Sergio Agüero]] latched onto a backpass from [[Danny Murphy (footballer, born 1977)|Danny Murphy]] and played the ball into [[Diego Forlán]], whose shot struck the post.<ref name="uefa_report">{{Cite web|first=John |last=Atkin |title=Forlán double gives Atlético glory |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f4f42d9b-b99f75c54493-1000--forlan-double-gives-atletico-glory/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=12 May 2010 |access-date=13 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100515035225/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/matches/season%3D2010/round%3D2000042/match%3D2000967/postmatch/report/index.html |archive-date=15 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> The sustained pressure from the Spanish club was eventually rewarded when a mistimed shot from Agüero fell to Forlán, who struck the ball past [[Mark Schwarzer]] to give Atlético a 1–0 lead. The lead, however, only lasted for five minutes, before [[Simon Davies (footballer, born 1979)|Simon Davies]] struck [[Zoltán Gera]]'s cross into the net on the [[Volley (football)|half-volley]] to level the scores at 1–1.<ref name="bbc_report"/>

In the second half, Fulham began to gain a foothold in the game and went close to taking the lead after an hour when Simon Davies struck a shot following a poor clearance, which required a save from Atlético goalkeeper [[David de Gea]]. In the 55th minute, [[Bobby Zamora]], who had been a fitness doubt prior to the game, was replaced by [[Clint Dempsey]], who became the first American to appear in a major European football final.<ref>{{Cite web|first=Stuart |last=Condie |title=Atletico wins Europa League; Dempsey in final |url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5iNrsHmlLb_BtMSS2GuT_xFJK_HvQD9FLHTI05 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=13 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100518042616/https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5iNrsHmlLb_BtMSS2GuT_xFJK_HvQD9FLHTI05 |archive-date=18 May 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Atlético finished the 90 minutes as the stronger side, but with neither team able to score, the game went into extra time.<ref name="uefa_report"/> In extra time, it was again Atlético who looked the more likely to score, with both Forlán and Agüero having opportunities to hit the net. As the tie approached 120 minutes, however, the Spanish side scored a winner, when Agüero turned Fulham defender [[Aaron Hughes]] and crossed the ball to Forlán, who flicked the ball into the goal.<ref name="bbc_report"/> Forlán was subsequently named man of the match for his performance.<ref name="forlan"/>

